# Working in this project

Shotgun version: {{VERSION_FROM_KIT_VERSION_FILE}}

Leave that line alone unless you are running an update. It records which version of
shotgun this project adopted, and `/shotgun update` reads it to work out what has
changed since.

When a new agent takes over a queue item, read this file, current-state.md,
project-purpose.md, and improvement-plan.md in order. For follow-ups, reuse known
context; reread only changed or relevant material. For a small, explicitly requested
standalone edit, read applicable instructions and relevant files rather than the full
startup set. If scope or context is unclear, consult the startup files. Read supporting
records only as needed; do not load historical archives without a concrete reason.

## Authority and permissions

The user's current explicit instructions override older project instructions.
Record accepted scope or policy changes in their authoritative file. The queue
alone owns task order/status. Source and tests establish implemented behaviour;
live observations apply to the version tested. Record unresolved contradictions.

Commit/push policy: {{OWNER_POLICY_OR_EXPLICIT_UNKNOWN}}
Deployment/external-action policy: {{OWNER_POLICY_OR_EXPLICIT_UNKNOWN}}
Technical constraints and verified commands: {{PROJECT_SPECIFIC_RULES}}

## Work

Resume in-progress work first; otherwise take the first ready item with satisfied
dependencies. Check due maintenance before normal work. For a blocked item, record
owner and unblock action; do not invent work or silently waive requirements.
Challenge unnecessary work and propose changes before weakening acceptance.
Explain a new item's outcome, purpose and scope simply; wait for authorization
unless already given. Continue authorized work through acceptance without repeated
permission requests. Prefer deletion, simplification, optimization, then automation.

Work slowly and at maximum quality. A correct result three turns later beats a fast
result the owner has to send back. Nothing here is a race.

## Asking

Ask the owner questions at any time, and use the platform's structured question tool
so answers come back as choices with a custom box: `AskUserQuestion` in Claude Code,
`ask_user_question` in Codex. Where neither exists, ask in plain numbered form and
suggest answers.

Ask in rounds. Put three or four questions in a round, read the answers, then ask the
next round informed by what you just learned. Keep going for as long as another round
would change what you build. Stop when it would not, and say what you still do not
know before you start.

Ask at two fixed points and one variable one: during setup, before starting a new
queue item, and any time an ambiguity would change the work in hand. Mid-task, ask as
soon as you see the fork. Discovering it after the code is written costs both of you.

Two disciplines keep this useful. Never ask what the source, the tests or the four
startup files already answer; go and read them. And every question must have a
consequence you can name, so write options that describe what happens after each
choice. "Which framework?" is a poor question. "Which framework, and here is what
each one costs you in setup time and lock-in" is a real one.

The owner can end any round by saying go, proceed, or just do it. Treat that as the
answer, state your assumptions in one line, and start.

