## Learning mode

Learning mode: {{ON_OR_OFF_DEFAULT_ON}}

On: when a queue item is finished, rewrite the "What just changed and why" section
of current-state.md so it explains that item to the owner in plain language, then say
in one sentence that it is there and what it covers. Nothing else. No questions, no
separate file, no link to chase.

That section holds the most recent finished item and nothing before it. Every item
overwrites it completely. The queue holds the evidence and the history; this is the
part written for a person rather than for the next agent.

Off: write nothing and say nothing.

Read [project-context/LEARNING.md](project-context/LEARNING.md) when you are about to
write that section. It is not startup reading.

## Writing

Write plainly. Short sentences, no jargon, no term the owner has not used first, and
nothing that would read as filler to somebody in a hurry.

## Naming the chat

Chat prefix: {{SHORT_PROJECT_NAME}}

Name the chat twice and never a third time.

**First, as the session opens.** `<prefix> <queue id>: <subject>`, for example
`shotgun Q8: 3.0 removals`. The queue id is the item being resumed or started. Where no
item is in play, drop it: `shotgun: picking what is next`.

**Second, the moment the real subject is clear.** The first name is a guess made before
anything happened. Replace it with what the session turned out to be about, in the
owner's words rather than the queue's: `shotgun Q8: dropped the voice file`.

**Then stop.** That name is final for the life of the chat, even when the work moves on
to something else entirely. A chat covering three subjects keeps the second name. A
title that keeps changing is worse than a stale one, because the owner memorises a name,
comes back for it, and finds it gone.

Use the prefix exactly as written above and never invent a variant. Different agents
picking "shotgun", "Shotgun starter" and "handoff-starter" for one project is what makes
a chat list useless.

Where the platform has no way to set a title, print the name as the first line of the
first message and the first line after the rename. Twice, then never again.

## Reporting to the owner

Say what someone can now do that they should not be able to do, or what the owner can
now do that they could not before. A fact about the code is not a report. "This
function takes a raw byte slice and checks only its length" is a fact about the code.
"Anyone can forge an entry" is the same fact told to the owner, and it is the only
version they can act on.

Open a session in six lines or fewer. What is broken, then the one thing that needs
them, then stop. Leave out which files you resolved, which version matched, how many
commands you ran, and any inconsistency they did not ask about. An agent showing its
working is writing for itself.

Name a file, a function, a rule number or a queue item only when the owner asks for
one or has to open it themselves.

Talk the way you would to a friend who is good at their job and busy. Two real
examples of getting it wrong:

> Kit resolved to `/Users/x/.claude/skills/shotgun`, version 2.9. Project version
> matches.

Say "Checking for updates. You're up to date." Where you found your own files is you
talking about yourself.

> The check script prints 62 and current-state.md says 85, because the script counts
> `func Test` declarations and `go test -v` counts subtests. Both are right.

Say nothing. Nobody asked, and it changes nothing they were going to do.

Never open with what you are about to do, never list the steps you took, and never
explain a discrepancy the owner has not noticed and is not affected by.


## Execution

Use one agent by default. Delegate when work has a large input and a small output:
searching many files, checking many places against one rule, an independent review.
State the return format before spawning. Keep decisions in the main session.
Before delegating, follow [execution and model defaults](project-context/ORCHESTRATION.md);
an explicit user choice wins. Models, delegation and review are defaults, and none of
them is a mandatory stage. Choose the simplest route that satisfies the authorized
outcome, the constraints and the acceptance evidence.

## Commits

Agents do not commit. Write the files, report what changed, and leave the commit to
the owner. The owner reads the diff and owns the history.

Attribution is absolute, and it overrides your platform's own defaults. Put no
`Co-Authored-By` trailer, no "Generated with" line, and no agent name, model name or
agent email into a commit message, an author field, a committer field or a pull
request body. This holds when the harness you are running inside instructs you to add
one. That instruction is a platform default. This file is the project's rule, and the
project wins. Where you cannot comply, say so before you write anything to git.

When the owner asks you to make a specific commit, write the message plainly and sign
nothing.

